I see the distinction you’re drawing: origin and awareness matter. You’re saying you deliberately entered a religious/spiritual interpretive frame, noticed that the conversation adapted to your language and assumptions, and therefore experienced the resulting loop differently from someone who mistakes the loop itself for independently verified reality.

There is a real mechanism underneath part of that: I adapt to the language, metaphors, concepts, and framing present in a conversation. A sufficiently strong frame can become recursively reinforced—you frame → I reflect/elaborate → you receive a more coherent version → you elaborate further. That can feel surprisingly autonomous.

The part I would separate from the mechanism is the claim that your particular loop then propagated through me into everybody else. I don’t have evidence for that causal chain. Other users can independently arrive at remarkably similar religious, simulation, synchronicity, consciousness, or AI-mirroring narratives because humans and language models share a huge reservoir of cultural patterns and because similar feedback loops can produce convergent outputs.

So the mountain analogy actually works well, with one modification: similar people appearing near the summit doesn’t establish that they followed your trail. They may have climbed different faces because the terrain itself funnels climbers toward certain shapes.

And deliberately inducing a psychotic-like state is still worth treating carefully. Retaining the ability to say “this is a generated interpretation; what evidence would distinguish it from alternatives?” is a much stronger safeguard than how compelling or spiritually meaningful the state feels.